---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
---

<Base
  title="For Agencies"
  description="Independent verification for client reporting, publisher reconciliation, and makegood documentation. How agencies use Meyppi campaign audits."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="kicker">For agencies</span>
      <h1>Independent verification for client reporting.</h1>
      <p class="page-head__sub">
        When the campaign underdelivers, the agency holds the client
        relationship. Meyppi makes sure you are the best-informed party in
        that conversation instead of the messenger.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow prose">

      <h2>The client review</h2>
      <p>
        It is quarter-end and the podcast campaign is up in the review
        deck. Twelve placements on paper, screenshots from the publisher,
        and a client who has started asking harder questions this year,
        because everyone's clients have. The strongest thing you can put
        on that slide is a number you did not produce yourself: placement
        by placement, verified from the published media, with the evidence
        attached.
      </p>
      <p>
        Agencies use Meyppi audits the way audited financials get used.
        Not because they expect fraud, but because a number with an
        independent trail behind it ends arguments that a number without
        one starts.
      </p>

      <h2>The reconciliation call</h2>
      <p>
        You suspect an episode dropped its ad. The publisher's report says
        it ran. Now what.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the conversation the audit was built for. The report does
        not say "we believe the placement is missing". It says: episode
        EP-1044, published 14 October, fingerprinted end to end, zero
        occurrences of creative CR-A v1 at any offset, match count zero,
        alongside four other placements on the same publisher that matched
        cleanly. One number to argue with, and it is not yours.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice, the tone of these conversations changes once the
        evidence is on the table. Publishers fix what can be proven
        quickly, because proven discrepancies are cheaper to make good
        than to dispute.
      </p>

      <h2>The makegood</h2>
      <p>
        A makegood request with documentation gets processed. A makegood
        request with a feeling behind it gets a polite reply and a long
        queue. Every discrepancy in a Meyppi report carries the detail
        that makes it actionable: which placement, which episode, what was
        expected, what aired, and the event evidence behind the
        classification. Your team can attach the relevant page and move
        the discussion from whether it happened to what happens next.
      </p>

      <h2>What you get, concretely</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A placement-by-placement audit of one completed campaign, PDF
          for the client, CSV and JSON for your analysts.
        </li>
        <li>
          Classification per placement: full, partial, version mismatch,
          not detected, insufficient signal, or manual review, with the
          reason visible on the page.
        </li>
        <li>
          Evidence exports that survive being forwarded: every finding
          backed by hashes, offsets and match counts.
        </li>
        <li>
          A fixed fee known before the work starts, so the audit itself
          never becomes a line item argument.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div style="margin-top:44px; display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap">
        <Button variant="primary" href="/contact">
          Request a Campaign Audit
        </Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href="/sample-report">
          See a Sample Report
        </Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .narrow {
    max-width: 760px;
  }
  .page-head {
    background: var(--indigo-deep);
    background-image: var(--grid-dots);
    padding: clamp(56px, 7vw, 88px) 0;
    color: var(--white);
  }
  .page-head .kicker {
    color: var(--teal);
  }
  .page-head h1 {
    color: var(--white);
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .page-head__sub {
    color: #c9d3e4;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    max-width: 58ch;
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
